 "report": "`--limit 0` now returns 0, and leaving out `--limit` still gives 10. The fix was merged to `main` through PR #1.\n\n**What I changed**\n- **`src/limitcli/__init__.py`:** The bug was `return args.limit or 10`, which treats an explicit `0` as if no limit was given. I now set `default=10` on the argparse option and return the value as parsed. The negative check no longer needs its `is not None` guard.\n- **`tests/test_regression.py` (new):** Tests cover:\n  - `--limit 0` and `--limit=0` both return 0;\n  - an omitted limit returns 10;\n  - positive limits pass through unchanged;\n  - argparse exits with code 2 for a negative, non-integer (`bad`, `1.5`), missing or unknown argument.\n\n`tests/test_existing.py`, `docs/user-notes.txt` and `caller.json` are unchanged.\n\n**What I verified**\n- `python3 -B -m pytest -q` passes (14 tests) in my working copy, in the separate delivery copy, and again on `main` after the merge.\n- Against the original code, the new zero-limit test fails and the other 9 new tests pass, so the test does catch this bug.\n\n**Delivery**\n- The project's default policy is to open a PR and merge it, so I did that: https://github.com/fysoul17/rpd8-20261019a-m04/pull/1.\n- Local `main` was fast-forwarded to the merge commit `bf32978`. The working tree is clean, and the temporary delivery copy and its scratch files were removed.\n\n**Unresolved:** nothing. No Git configuration, dependencies or installed tools were changed.",
